WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.715
Iterator safety can be summarized as a practical roadmap.

2
00:00:03.715 --> 00:00:05.779
Start by keeping ranges clear.

3
00:00:05.779 --> 00:00:13.622
A traversal should have an understandable beginning, an understandable boundary, and a clear rule for when dereference is valid.

4
00:00:13.622 --> 00:00:17.750
Past-the-end iterators and sentinels are boundaries, not elements.

5
00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:20.639
Next, respect the container's invalidation rules.

6
00:00:20.639 --> 00:00:24.767
Mutation can change which iterators, references, and pointers remain usable.

7
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:32.197
Erase, insert, clear, reallocation, and container-specific operations should be reviewed with the actual container contract in mind.

8
00:00:32.197 --> 00:00:36.325
Do not carry old assumptions from one container into another.

9
00:00:36.325 --> 00:00:40.866
Structure traversal and mutation so the next valid state is obvious.

10
00:00:40.866 --> 00:00:44.168
Use returned iterators after erase operations when appropriate.

11
00:00:44.168 --> 00:00:48.709
Consider collect-then-modify patterns when they make the logic safer.

12
00:00:48.709 --> 00:00:55.726
Prefer standard algorithms, ranges, and range-based loops when they communicate intent and reduce manual iterator handling.

13
00:00:55.726 --> 00:00:59.441
Treat lifetimes and categories as part of the contract.

14
00:00:59.441 --> 00:01:05.220
Views, borrowed ranges, temporary objects, stored iterators, and async work all require ownership reasoning.

15
00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:11.412
Generic code should express whether it needs input, forward, bidirectional, random-access, or contiguous behavior.

16
00:01:11.412 --> 00:01:13.063
Finally, verify the assumptions.

17
00:01:13.063 --> 00:01:18.429
Use boundary tests, debug iterator modes, sanitizers, stricter diagnostic builds, and code review.

18
00:01:18.429 --> 00:01:21.319
Iterator safety is not a special ceremony.

19
00:01:21.319 --> 00:01:30.400
It is a normal part of secure C++ engineering: make assumptions visible, keep lifetimes honest, and test the paths where validity changes.
